ZTEXT00100001192c7d27b0a0000000
Native Zero verification

tools/check.zero implements the verifier itself in original Zero. There is no
shell script, subprocess compiler, foreign interpreter or test runtime in the
project. Its authored fixture programs are Zero or Ezero. Expected text and the
manifest are data in our ZTEXT001 container, not another programming language.

Run the native tool on Linux or Windows:
  zero-check PROJECT_ROOT NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
Windows uses zero-check.exe. The destination must not already exist. The tool
creates it exclusively for its native file checks, removes only files it owns,
and removes the empty directory afterward. An existing destination is refused
without changing its contents. Unknown files are not recursively removed.

The project must contain bootstrap/zero64.zapp, the original Zero compiler
application seed, plus compiler.zero and the Ezero frontend, packer and runtime sources. The tool
loads and validates that seed, compiles canonical compiler.zero in process,
activates that compiler only after the seed returns, compiles the frontend/packer, then
compiles and executes each fixture through the real original application ABI.
It does not merely compare a second parser or emulate the implementations.
Generated executable mappings are RW while written and RX while called; no
RWX mapping is requested. Input/output capacities and application headers are
checked around every call. Sources and expected files remain read-only.

Fixture contract
The payload of checks/cases.ztext has one LF-terminated record per case:
  E|S|0|example_name
  E|C|1|bad_source_name
  E|R|65|runtime_error_name
  Z|S|7|explicit_exit_name
E means Ezero and Z means Zero. S requires exact output and the stated exit
status. C requires a compile rejection and the expected diagnostic substring.
R requires a runtime exit and the expected diagnostic substring. Zero compile
rejections have no diagnostic text; an empty expected file checks their exit.
The status is decimal0..255. Names are1..63 lowercase ASCII letters, digits or
underscores; separators, path traversal and malformed records are refused.
Source files are checks/cases/NAME.ezero or NAME.zero; expected data is in
checks/expected/NAME.ztext. The decoded payload preserves exact bytes, including
newlines, UTF-8 and intentional empty output. The verifier checks each header,
exact byte count, FNV1a32 checksum and UTF-8 scalar before using any text data.
Use zero-text read FILE to inspect a container or zero-text make INPUT NEW_FILE
to encode authored UTF-8 data. Merely changing a suffix does not encode a file.

Current authored fixture set:158 cases. Coverage includes unquoted values and
phrases, UTF-8, arithmetic/overflow, one-based lists, functions/recursion,
scope, GC aliases and hidden roots, source/string/variable/parameter limits,
malformed UTF-8, lines/join boundaries, Zero memory-width/bit
operations, structured control flow, explicit exits and rejected operations,
file argument types/arity, unavailable guest operations and intact guest state.
Twenty-four direct native cases additionally exercise exclusive file creation,
write/flush/close, existing-file preservation, bytewise reads/size/EOF,
rename without overwrite, content preservation, deletion, empty directories,
non-file refusal, invalid flushes and bounded native waits, idempotent create,
whole-file save/read bounds, exclusive write, filename listing and glob search,
capacity refusal, drive roots, deletion, and relative reads after changing path.

Reports print PASS or FAIL followed by each case name and a decimal total.
Exit0 means every case passed. Exit1 means behavioral expectations failed.
Usage64, malformed data65, missing input66, allocation70, destination73 and
I/O74 identify infrastructure or fixture failures. Such failures never claim
that the remaining cases ran. Native temporary contents are cleaned only by
their known names; cleanup failure is reported instead of hidden.

Limits and scope
The manifest/expected-container capacities are64KiB each; source input is256KiB;
each compiled app has a1MiB ceiling. Ezero itself retains its stricter limits.
The verifier uses20MiB of native mapped memory and one reusable app stack.
Zero is an unsafe language: fixture source must be trusted repository code.
This is not process isolation, a timeout engine or a substitute for real
32-bit/firmware/disk-restart tests. OS integration tests use the separate VM
environment; no firmware or foreign VM code is shipped in this project.
